WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.535
This briefing comes from
Devices and Endpoints,

3
00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:06.725
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:10.284
It's about moving devices
from Group Policy to Intune,

5
00:00:10.284 --> 00:00:12.387
and moving servers to Azure Arc.

6
00:00:12.387 --> 00:00:14.775
The question in the
title sounds technical,

7
00:00:14.775 --> 00:00:17.362
but the answer turns out
to be about governance.

8
00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:20.863
Group Policy did a lot of quiet
work that nobody wrote down,

9
00:00:20.863 --> 00:00:23.488
and that work doesn't
come along for the ride.

10
00:00:23.488 --> 00:00:28.010
So we'll go in four steps: why Group Policy
can't simply be carried to the cloud,

11
00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.343
what replaces it, who owns the endpoint,

12
00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:34.938
and how to migrate a fleet without losing
what Group Policy quietly gave you.

13
00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:36.750
Here's the short version up front.

14
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.600
This isn't a settings migration.

15
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.175
It's a governance rebuild.

16
00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:43.260
First, where this book sits in the series.

17
00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.531
Here's why this matters: the series
moves four populations to the cloud,

18
00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:54.960
each on its own track, and
this book carries two of them.

19
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.447
The Intune track is end-user devices,
and the Arc track is servers.

20
00:00:59.447 --> 00:01:04.696
A device's attributes come from device
provisioning, like the Autopilot group tag,

21
00:01:04.696 --> 00:01:07.860
never from HR, and a device
rule can't read its owner.

22
00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.936
Devices are done when their policy
targets those attributes and

23
00:01:11.936 --> 00:01:15.710
the computer-side GPO links are
gone, one population at a time.

24
00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:21.235
Servers are done when each is Arc-enabled,
tagged, and evaluated by Azure Policy.

25
00:01:21.235 --> 00:01:24.650
And Group Policy splits: user
settings go with people,

26
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.522
computer settings with
devices and servers.

27
00:01:27.522 --> 00:01:30.720
First, which platforms each one reaches.

28
00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:39.595
Here's why this matters: an inventory that
only covers Windows isn't an inventory.

29
00:01:39.595 --> 00:01:42.533
Intune isn't just for Microsoft platforms.

30
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:45.616
It manages Windows, Apple
and Android devices,

31
00:01:45.616 --> 00:01:50.483
and Linux desktops with limits, while
ChromeOS stays in Google's console.

32
00:01:50.483 --> 00:01:54.786
Arc reaches Windows and Linux
servers wherever they run:

33
00:01:54.786 --> 00:01:59.182
on premises, VMware VMs, and
AWS and Google Cloud instances.

34
00:01:59.182 --> 00:02:01.370
Azure VMs don't need Arc at all.

35
00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:07.147
For what neither reaches, like printers,
network gear, the mainframe or old Unix,

36
00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:12.301
every device still lands in one of four
tiers: Intune or Arc; its own tool,

37
00:02:12.301 --> 00:02:15.056
with the CMDB holding its attributes;

38
00:02:15.056 --> 00:02:19.233
discovered and monitored only;
or segmented and retired.

39
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:21.557
A device in no tier is a finding.

40
00:02:21.557 --> 00:02:25.050
Now, why Group Policy
can't simply come along.

41
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.062
Here's the core problem.

42
00:02:30.062 --> 00:02:34.849
Group Policy governed by position,
and an Intune device has no position.

43
00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:40.775
Where a computer sat in the organizational
unit (OU) tree quietly did five jobs.

44
00:02:40.775 --> 00:02:44.807
It decided which settings applied,
which setting won, who could change it,

45
00:02:44.807 --> 00:02:48.100
when it refreshed, and what
evidence you had of what applied.

46
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.562
An Entra-joined, Intune-managed
device has none of that.

47
00:02:51.562 --> 00:02:55.237
Policy goes flatly to groups,
and nothing reads the tree.

48
00:02:55.237 --> 00:02:57.571
When two policies set the same thing,

49
00:02:57.571 --> 00:03:00.838
Intune reports a conflict
instead of resolving it.

50
00:03:00.838 --> 00:03:05.074
Some jobs, like mapped drives, have
no built-in equivalent at all.

51
00:03:05.074 --> 00:03:09.275
That's why Microsoft advises
against copying GPOs one for one.

52
00:03:09.275 --> 00:03:11.770
Teams that do it find
the governance gap only

53
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.400
after the targeting model
has already changed.

54
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.488
Every job the tree did implicitly
has to be rebuilt explicitly.

55
00:03:18.488 --> 00:03:20.750
So what about the obvious shortcut?

56
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.550
The obvious shortcut is hybrid
join, and the book calls it a trap.

57
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.875
Microsoft's guidance is two-sided.

58
00:03:30.875 --> 00:03:35.150
New or reset Windows devices
should be Entra joined by default.

59
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:40.500
Existing domain-joined devices can be
hybrid joined, with little impact on users.

60
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.450
But Microsoft is clear that hybrid join
isn't meant to be the long-term end state.

61
00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.337
The trap is how easy it looks.

62
00:03:47.337 --> 00:03:51.262
Turning it on is close to a single
tenant-wide switch, so something

63
00:03:51.262 --> 00:03:55.624
assessments treat as a multi-quarter
program can look finished in a week.

64
00:03:55.624 --> 00:03:59.437
Meanwhile the fleet keeps every
on-premises dependency it had.

65
00:03:59.437 --> 00:04:02.251
And there's no supported
way to convert a hybrid

66
00:04:02.251 --> 00:04:05.137
device to Entra join
without resetting Windows.

67
00:04:05.137 --> 00:04:10.024
So the governed path uses hybrid join
where it helps, then moves each device

68
00:04:10.024 --> 00:04:14.438
to Entra join at a natural event,
like a hardware refresh or a rebuild.

69
00:04:14.438 --> 00:04:17.430
Now, what actually replaces Group Policy?

70
00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.704
What replaces it depends
on what you're managing,

71
00:04:23.704 --> 00:04:26.355
because devices and servers
take different roads.

72
00:04:26.355 --> 00:04:30.233
For endpoints, the Entra device
object carries the identity,

73
00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:33.257
Intune carries configuration
and compliance,

74
00:04:33.257 --> 00:04:37.755
Autopilot does zero-touch provisioning,
and Defender covers threats.

75
00:04:37.755 --> 00:04:41.518
Servers are different, and the old
design documents got this wrong.

76
00:04:41.518 --> 00:04:43.979
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

77
00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:48.728
A server comes under governance through
Azure Arc, which makes it an Azure resource,

78
00:04:48.728 --> 00:04:52.443
and Azure Policy machine
configuration sets its configuration.

79
00:04:52.443 --> 00:04:56.767
Patching and protection come from Update
Manager and Defender for Servers.

80
00:04:56.767 --> 00:04:59.806
Each of these systems is
authoritative for one thing.

81
00:04:59.806 --> 00:05:02.540
So the program ties
them together on stable

82
00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.655
keys and reconciles the
inventory continuously.

83
00:05:05.655 --> 00:05:09.032
That way the fleet has one
answer to a simple question:

84
00:05:09.032 --> 00:05:11.105
what do we have, and is it governed?

85
00:05:11.105 --> 00:05:14.330
For brand-new devices,
the book goes further.

86
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.428
For new devices, the
doctrine is Intune-first,

87
00:05:21.428 --> 00:05:26.043
and the headline is the slide
title: no ungoverned device, ever.

88
00:05:26.043 --> 00:05:30.130
Every device is enrolled in governance
before it's used in production.

89
00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.080
Governance starts at procurement,
so the device's organizational

90
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.030
assignment is set when the order is
placed, not when the box arrives.

91
00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.517
That's the same idea as HR
feeding user provisioning.

92
00:05:41.517 --> 00:05:46.010
Enrollment is zero-touch, and
manual enrollment is a justified,

93
00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.343
time-limited, audited exception.

94
00:05:48.343 --> 00:05:50.912
Each asset gets one management plane,

95
00:05:50.912 --> 00:05:53.980
and there's no new Group
Policy or hybrid join.

96
00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.101
And if onboarding fails, the device
goes to quarantine with a ticket,

97
00:05:58.101 --> 00:06:00.530
never into production half-configured.

98
00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:05.294
One practical note for GCC tenants:
the book defaults to classic Autopilot,

99
00:06:05.294 --> 00:06:08.368
because manufacturer
registration can carry a

100
00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:11.442
group tag and the newer
device preparation can't.

101
00:06:11.442 --> 00:06:13.181
That covers new devices.

102
00:06:13.181 --> 00:06:16.290
The existing fleet takes
one of three paths.

103
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.428
There are three ways to get the existing
fleet to the same governed state,

104
00:06:24.428 --> 00:06:26.402
and the book uses each on purpose.

105
00:06:26.402 --> 00:06:29.614
The governed path is
orchestrated by the program,

106
00:06:29.614 --> 00:06:32.827
with every step recorded,
approved and evidenced.

107
00:06:32.827 --> 00:06:33.953
It's the default.

108
00:06:33.953 --> 00:06:36.815
The manual path uses the
native tools by hand,

109
00:06:36.815 --> 00:06:40.141
for when the orchestration
platform isn't ready yet.

110
00:06:40.141 --> 00:06:44.015
It works, but more of the evidence
has to be collected by hand.

111
00:06:44.015 --> 00:06:47.260
And the retrofit path takes
an estate someone already

112
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.153
modernized by hand and brings it
under governance after the fact.

113
00:06:51.153 --> 00:06:56.253
All three end in the same place:
enrolled, baselined and reconciled.

114
00:06:56.253 --> 00:06:59.428
The difference is how much of
the proof comes automatically.

115
00:06:59.428 --> 00:07:02.915
The chapter also fixes a few
errors in the old guides.

116
00:07:02.915 --> 00:07:07.209
For example, the highest LAN
Manager authentication level

117
00:07:07.209 --> 00:07:11.153
still accepts NTLM version
two, so it doesn't block NTLM.

118
00:07:11.153 --> 00:07:13.280
Next, who owns all this?

119
00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:20.318
Ownership matters because the
work crosses several teams,

120
00:07:20.318 --> 00:07:23.405
so the book separates who
decides from who does the work.

121
00:07:23.405 --> 00:07:30.180
On the deciding side, the CISO and ISSO
own the baselines, exceptions and evidence.

122
00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.130
The endpoint engineering lead
owns policy design and rollout.

123
00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:39.305
The change board owns the update rings,
the migration waves and any pause.

124
00:07:39.305 --> 00:07:44.558
On the doing side, endpoint engineering
builds the Intune policies and Autopilot

125
00:07:44.558 --> 00:07:47.725
profiles, the server team
onboards servers to Arc,

126
00:07:47.725 --> 00:07:51.355
and security operations runs
Defender and the response.

127
00:07:51.355 --> 00:07:54.970
Application owners, the help
desk and pilot users are

128
00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.667
consulted and affected; pilot
users tell us what broke.

129
00:07:58.667 --> 00:08:03.205
And one rule applies to people as
much as devices: least privilege.

130
00:08:03.205 --> 00:08:08.317
Daily work runs on scoped Intune roles,
not the broad Intune Administrator role,

131
00:08:08.317 --> 00:08:11.492
which Microsoft advises
against for routine use.

132
00:08:11.492 --> 00:08:14.900
With owners named, here's
how the sunset runs.

133
00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:22.903
The Group Policy sunset runs as
a program, one setting at a time,

134
00:08:22.903 --> 00:08:25.988
in seven steps from
inventory to retirement.

135
00:08:25.988 --> 00:08:29.824
Every setting ends one of three
ways: migrate it to Intune,

136
00:08:29.824 --> 00:08:33.251
replace it with a cloud-native
pattern, or retire it.

137
00:08:33.251 --> 00:08:37.300
The coexistence rules matter most,
and the old guides had them wrong.

138
00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:41.425
While a device is under both,
Group Policy wins by default.

139
00:08:41.425 --> 00:08:45.850
There's a setting that flips that, but it
only covers part of the policy surface.

140
00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.858
Defender settings and
Windows Hello for Business

141
00:08:48.858 --> 00:08:51.938
have to be removed from
Group Policy, not overridden.

142
00:08:51.938 --> 00:08:57.150
Group Policy preferences can leave
settings behind after a GPO is unlinked,

143
00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.549
so each one needs a planned cleanup.

144
00:08:59.549 --> 00:09:04.550
And populations are unlinked one at a
time, because that's scoped and reversible.

145
00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.975
Finally, measure, don't assume.

146
00:09:06.975 --> 00:09:10.863
The program runs Group Policy
analytics on its own exports.

147
00:09:10.863 --> 00:09:13.210
Then the fleet itself migrates.

148
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.719
The existing fleet
migrates in seven phases,

149
00:09:19.719 --> 00:09:23.223
about thirty-four weeks on
the book's planning estimates.

150
00:09:23.223 --> 00:09:28.308
It runs from inventory and coexistence
planning, through baselines and a

151
00:09:28.308 --> 00:09:33.560
small pilot, to cutover in waves,
verification, and finally retiring the GPOs.

152
00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.329
Each wave is watched before
the next one starts,

153
00:09:36.329 --> 00:09:39.523
and old GPOs are kept for a
while before they're deleted.

154
00:09:39.523 --> 00:09:42.161
The slide has the details for each phase.

155
00:09:42.161 --> 00:09:46.910
The book also sets a floor:
Windows 11 version 24H2.

156
00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.947
Windows 10 is already out of support,

157
00:09:49.947 --> 00:09:56.123
and Windows 11 23H2 Enterprise stops
getting updates on November 10, 2026.

158
00:09:56.123 --> 00:10:00.534
Keep in mind these are planning estimates
for a large fleet, not promises.

159
00:10:00.534 --> 00:10:03.322
Every gate is the next
phase's precondition,

160
00:10:03.322 --> 00:10:06.110
and every phase can roll
back while it's open.

161
00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.447
That's the devices.

162
00:10:07.447 --> 00:10:10.990
Servers have their own road,
and their own deadlines.

163
00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.497
Servers come under governance
through Azure Arc.

164
00:10:17.497 --> 00:10:21.263
Onboarding uses the Connected
Machine agent with least privilege:

165
00:10:21.263 --> 00:10:25.365
a certificate-based identity that's
needed only when the server connects.

166
00:10:25.365 --> 00:10:29.577
Configuration comes from Azure Policy
machine configuration baselines,

167
00:10:29.577 --> 00:10:33.790
and patching and protection from Update
Manager and Defender for Servers.

168
00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:35.453
Two dates make this urgent.

169
00:10:35.453 --> 00:10:43.095
Extended security updates for Windows Server
2012 and 2012 R2 ended on October 13,

170
00:10:43.095 --> 00:10:47.815
2026, so any 2012 host still
running is a red finding.

171
00:10:47.815 --> 00:10:54.165
And Windows Server 2016 reaches
end of support on January 12, 2027.

172
00:10:54.165 --> 00:10:56.477
The book is careful about rollback too.

173
00:10:56.477 --> 00:11:01.071
Remove extensions before you disconnect
the agent, because disconnecting

174
00:11:01.071 --> 00:11:05.591
deletes the Azure resource, and the
onboarding role isn't allowed to do that.

175
00:11:05.591 --> 00:11:08.510
Last, how do we run all this and prove it?

176
00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:15.997
Once the fleet is moved, the
job is to run it and prove it.

177
00:11:15.997 --> 00:11:20.886
Device compliance feeds Conditional
Access, which Access and Privilege covers.

178
00:11:20.886 --> 00:11:24.346
Updates flow through rings
and Windows Autopatch,

179
00:11:24.346 --> 00:11:29.785
and in a GCC tenant the update evidence
comes from Intune and Autopatch reports.

180
00:11:29.785 --> 00:11:32.971
The inventory is reconciled
across all the systems,

181
00:11:32.971 --> 00:11:35.862
devices are wiped before
accounts are deleted,

182
00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:41.197
and the evidence maps to the configuration-management
and flaw-remediation controls.

183
00:11:41.197 --> 00:11:43.473
From leadership, we need four things.

184
00:11:43.473 --> 00:11:46.385
Approve Intune-first for every new device.

185
00:11:46.385 --> 00:11:49.885
Fund the Group Policy
inventory and analytics run.

186
00:11:49.885 --> 00:11:55.585
Set the Windows 11 24H2 floor and a plan
for servers reaching end of support.

187
00:11:55.585 --> 00:11:59.397
And name the owners for
endpoints, servers and security.

188
00:11:59.397 --> 00:12:05.098
Devices and Endpoints has the full migration,
with every external claim footnoted.

189
00:12:05.098 --> 00:12:08.822
Access and Privilege covers what
a compliant device can reach,

190
00:12:08.822 --> 00:12:11.973
and Identity and Directory
the identity underneath.

191
00:12:11.973 --> 00:12:12.790
Thank you.
